  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 147 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nAs the upper trough becomes established over the eastern half of the \nUS, a high amplitude ridge forms over the Great Plains in its wake, \nsandwiched between the eastern trough and another trough over the \nWest Coast. Our area will gradually shift from being under the \ninfluence of the trough to being more under the influence of the \nridge. At the surface, the surface high will have its greatest \ninfluence over our area this weekend yielding light winds and cold \nweather. Despite greater sun on Friday, temperatures only reach the \nmid 40s for highs. By Friday night, we should see a pretty solid \nradiational cooling set up allowing the cold spots to experience a \nhard freeze in the low 20s. Saturday night could be just as cold \ndepending on whether clouds begin to spill into the region.\n\nAfter this, we'll see a gradual warm up back toward normal \ntemperatures for this time of year, in the 50s for highs and near \nfreezing for lows, through the middle of next week. In prior \nforecasts, ensemble guidance was more variable in how quickly we \ntransitioned out of the cold air mass and into the warmth of the \nridge. However, latest guidance is in better agreement on the idea \nof the gradual moderating trend locally. That increased confidence \nis shown in the NBM Interquartile Range for high temperatures which \nhas fallen to 5 degrees or less each day through Wednesday, cutting \nthe variance in half relative to 24 hours ago.\n\nRain chances remain minimal through this 7-day forecast period. The \nbest opportunity is from a southern stream shortwave that tracks out \nof the Four Corners and toward the Gulf Coast Sunday into Monday, \nundercutting the ridge to its north. Trends continue to keep this \nwell south of our area, leaving us with only some high level \ncloudiness. Latest 12Z LREF guidance shows less than 20 percent of \nmembers with measurable rain in our area. Latest NBM is still a bit \nhigher with the PoP Sunday night as it is slower to respond to \ntrends in guidance.",
